const e=[{id:"sv-3-731",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A team's alternative hypothesis is **Hₐ: μ < 12 min**. What kind of test is this?",options:["A two-tailed test with α split between both tails","A one-tailed test with all of α in the upper tail","A two-tailed test with all of α in the lower tail","A one-tailed test with all of α in the lower tail"],answer:3,hint:"The sign in Hₐ tells you how many tails, and which one.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"≠ in Hₐ → two-tailed; < or > → one-tailed, with all of α in the tail that Hₐ points to."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Hₐ says **<**, so only a result far in the **lower** tail supports it."}],final:"< in Hₐ → one-tailed, lower tail → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a two-tailed test belongs to an alternative written with ≠, not with <.","Incorrect — the upper tail is used when Hₐ says >, a claim that the mean has increased.","Incorrect — a two-tailed test always splits α between the tails; it never puts it all in one.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-732",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book reminds Belts that making a decision does not fix a problem; taking [?] does.`,options:["action","approval","measurements","samples"],answer:0,hint:"What turns a statistical decision into a real improvement?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book: "Making a decision does not FIX a problem, taking action does."'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A test only tells you whether an X matters; the improvement comes from acting on it in Improve and Control."}],final:"Decision ≠ fix; action fixes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — sponsor approval authorises work but does not change the process.","Incorrect — more measurements give more evidence, but evidence alone does not fix anything.","Incorrect — sampling feeds the test; the problem stays until something is changed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-733",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **test statistic** in a hypothesis test is CORRECT?",options:["It is the probability that the null hypothesis is true","It is chosen by the team before any data are collected","It is always equal to α when H₀ is rejected","It measures how far the sample result lies from the H₀ value, in standard-error units"],answer:3,hint:"t or Z: signal divided by noise.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A test statistic (t, Z, F, χ²) expresses the gap between the sample result and the H₀ value relative to its standard error."},{title:"Apply",body:"The further it falls into the tail of its reference distribution, the smaller the p-value."}],final:"Statistic = distance from H₀ in SE units → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — no test output gives the probability that H₀ is true; p is calculated assuming H₀.","Incorrect — α is chosen beforehand; the test statistic is calculated from the data.","Incorrect — α is a probability set in advance; the statistic is compared with a critical value.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-734",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team tests mean **link jitter** against a 20 ms target (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
One-Sample T: Jitter_ms
Test of μ = 20 vs ≠ 20

 N   Mean   StDev  SE Mean     95% CI           T      P
25  21.10   2.60    0.52   (20.03, 22.17)   2.12  0.045
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is worded CORRECTLY?`,options:["There is not sufficient evidence at the 0.05 level to show that mean jitter differs from 20 ms","The test proves that the mean jitter of all links is 21.10 ms","H₀ is accepted because 20 ms lies close to the confidence interval","There is sufficient evidence at the 0.05 level to show that mean jitter differs from 20 ms"],answer:3,hint:"Compare p with α, then use the book's conclusion template.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"p = 0.045 < 0.05, and the 95% CI (20.03, 22.17) does not contain 20 → reject H₀."},{title:"Word it",body:'Template: "There is sufficient evidence at the α level of significance to show that [Hₐ]" — here, the mean differs from 20 ms.'}],final:"p < α → sufficient evidence of a difference → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p = 0.045 is below 0.05 and 20 lies outside the CI, so H₀ is rejected.","Incorrect — 21.10 is a sample estimate, and a test never proves anything.",'Incorrect — "accept H₀" is never correct wording, and 20 is outside the interval.',""],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-735",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:`A team planned a two-tailed test of whether mean install time differs from 5 days. After seeing that the sample mean is **above** 5 days, the Black Belt switches to a one-tailed test so that the result becomes significant.

Was this an appropriate action?`,options:["Yes, because a one-tailed test is more powerful in the observed direction","Yes, because the data now show which direction to test","No, because the tail must be chosen from the practical question before the data are seen","No, because one-tailed tests are never used in Six Sigma projects"],answer:2,hint:"Which comes first — the question or the data?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"≠, < or > in Hₐ comes from the practical problem at the statistical-problem step, before data collection."},{title:"Apply",body:"Switching tails after looking at the data puts all of α where the data already point, so the stated α is no longer true."}],final:"Tail fixed by the question in advance → No → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the extra power is only legitimate when the direction is set by the question in advance.","Incorrect — letting the data choose the tail inflates the real Type I risk.","","Incorrect — one-tailed tests are valid when the question is directional, such as 'has it increased?'."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-736",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:`When H₀ is not rejected, the book's conclusion reads: *"There is not sufficient evidence at the α level of significance to show that [Hₐ]."*`,options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Recall the book's two conclusion templates.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Reject H₀: "There **is** sufficient evidence…". Fail to reject: "There is **not** sufficient evidence…".'},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement quotes the fail-to-reject wording correctly."}],final:"The fail-to-reject template is correct → True → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this is exactly the book's fail-to-reject template; it avoids claiming that H₀ is true."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-737",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A product manager asks whether new modem firmware has **increased** mean download throughput above the current **85 Mbps**. Which pair of hypotheses and tail matches the question?",options:["H₀: μ = 85, Hₐ: μ ≠ 85; two-tailed","H₀: μ > 85, Hₐ: μ = 85; one-tailed, upper tail","H₀: μ = 85, Hₐ: μ < 85; one-tailed, lower tail","H₀: μ = 85, Hₐ: μ > 85; one-tailed, upper tail"],answer:3,hint:"Put the claim in Hₐ; its sign picks the tail.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"H₀ holds the equal sign (no change); Hₐ holds the claim. A directional claim gives a one-tailed test."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:'"Increased" → Hₐ: μ > 85, so all of α sits in the upper tail.'}],final:"Claim of increase → μ > 85, upper tail → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — ≠ asks about a change in either direction; the manager only asks about an increase.","Incorrect — the equal sign always belongs in H₀, and the claim goes in Hₐ.","Incorrect — the lower tail tests a decrease, the opposite of the claim.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-738",phase:3,topic:"3.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How is the decision made with the **critical-value route** of a hypothesis test?",options:["By rejecting H₀ when the test statistic falls between the two critical values","By rejecting H₀ when the test statistic falls beyond the critical value, in the rejection region","By rejecting H₀ when the p-value is larger than α","By rejecting H₀ when the sample mean equals the hypothesised value"],answer:1,hint:"The critical value marks the edge of the α region.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The critical value cuts off an area of α (the rejection region). A test statistic inside that tail area leads to rejecting H₀."},{title:"Link",body:"It always gives the same decision as the p-value route: statistic in the region ⇔ p < α."}],final:"Statistic in the rejection region → reject H₀ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the area between the critical values is where H₀ is not rejected.","","Incorrect — that inverts the p-value rule; H₀ is rejected when p is smaller than α.","Incorrect — a sample mean equal to the H₀ value is the least evidence against H₀."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–294"},{id:"sv-3-739",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does the book mean by **practical significance**?",options:["A difference unlikely to have occurred by chance at the chosen α","A difference with enough economic or technical value to pay for making the change","The probability of seeing data this extreme if H₀ were true","The probability of detecting a real difference when one exists"],answer:1,hint:"Practical = does it matter to the business?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: practical significance is the improvement that has practical, economic or technical value — enough to pay for the cost of making it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Chance, p-value and power are statistical ideas; none says whether the change is worth money."}],final:"Practical = worth the cost → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is statistical significance.","","Incorrect — that is the p-value.","Incorrect — that is the power of the test (1 − β)."],bookRef:"Book pp. 269, 285"},{id:"sv-3-740",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does a **statistically significant** result tell a Black Belt?",options:["That the difference is large enough to justify its cost","That the X has been proved to be the root cause","That the observed difference probably did not occur by chance","That the measurement system is adequate for the study"],answer:2,hint:"Statistical significance is about chance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: statistical significance is a change that probably, with a defined confidence, did not happen by chance."},{title:"Apply",body:"It says the difference is likely real — not that it is big, valuable or causal."}],final:"Statistical = probably not chance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is practical significance, a business judgement.","Incorrect — tests prove nothing, and root cause is a separate check.","","Incorrect — measurement adequacy is judged by MSA in Measure, not by a test result."],bookRef:"Book pp. 269, 285"},{id:"sv-3-741",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"With α = 0.05, when is a test result called **statistically significant**?",options:["When the p-value is greater than 0.05","When the power is less than 0.05","When the test statistic is less than 0.05","When the p-value is less than 0.05"],answer:3,hint:"Which number from the output is compared with α?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Decision rule: p < α → reject H₀ → statistically significant."},{title:"Apply",body:"With α = 0.05, any p below 0.05 is significant."}],final:"p < α → significant → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p above α means fail to reject H₀; nothing significant has been shown.","Incorrect — power is the chance of detecting a real difference and is not compared with α.","Incorrect — the test statistic is compared with a critical value, not with α itself.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-742",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does a **p-value** measure?",options:["The probability of a difference at least this large by chance if H₀ were true","The probability that the null hypothesis is true","The size of the difference between the population means","The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true"],answer:0,hint:"p is calculated under an assumption — which one?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The p-value is the chance of getting a difference this large by chance alone **if H₀ were true**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"It is not P(H₀ true), not P(Hₐ true) and not an effect size."}],final:"p = chance of this result if H₀ is true → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a classic misreading; p is calculated assuming H₀ is true.","Incorrect — size is shown by the estimate and its confidence interval, not by p.","Incorrect — p gives no direct probability for Hₐ either."],trap:"A tiny p-value can belong to a tiny, worthless difference.",bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-743",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which significance level does the book describe as the **most common** choice?",options:["0.01","0.05","0.10","0.20"],answer:1,hint:"Think of the default α in most worked examples.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book table: 0.05 most common (typical in manufacturing), 0.10 transactional, 0.01 rare."},{title:"Apply",body:"The default is 0.05."}],final:"Most common α = 0.05 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book keeps 0.01 for rare cases where H₀ must be very hard to reject.","","Incorrect — the book uses 0.10 for transactional projects involving human behaviour.","Incorrect — 0.20 is not one of the book's usual α values; it would give many false alarms."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-744",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, how is the ability to detect a difference related to **sample size**?",options:["Sample size affects practical significance but never statistical significance","It is directly linked: larger samples can detect smaller differences","Larger samples are needed only when the difference to detect is large","Sample size matters only for attribute data, not for continuous data"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the book's n = 2 versus n = 30 picture.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Book: "Our ability to detect a difference is directly linked to sample size."'},{title:"Apply",body:"A bigger n shrinks the standard error, so smaller differences stand out from chance."}],final:"Detection directly linked to n → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the reverse: n changes the statistical result; practical value is set by the business.","","Incorrect — large differences need small samples; it is small differences that need large ones.","Incorrect — the book's illustration (n = 2 vs n = 30) uses continuous data."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-745",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the **delta (δ)** that the book uses when sizing a test?",options:["The sample standard deviation of the individual values","The probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis","The size of the difference between two means, or between a mean and a target","The width of the confidence interval for the mean"],answer:2,hint:"δ is a difference, S is a spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: delta = the size of the difference between two means or between a mean and a target."},{title:"Apply",body:"δ is set from what matters to the business; detection depends on δ compared with S."}],final:"δ = the difference to detect → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is sigma (S), the other half of the δ-to-S ratio.","Incorrect — that is α, the Type I risk.","","Incorrect — CI width depends on S and n; δ is the difference you want to detect."],bookRef:"Book pp. 290, 299"},{id:"sv-3-746",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Two planned studies must detect the **same** difference. Study X has a much larger standard deviation than study Y. What does the book say about study X?",options:["It needs a smaller sample because the spread is larger","It will always give a smaller p-value than study Y","It is more likely to be practically significant","It needs a larger sample to establish the difference"],answer:3,hint:"Large S compared with δ makes differences hard to see.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: with a large S, differences are hard to establish and larger samples are needed to reduce uncertainty."},{title:"Apply",body:"Same δ, bigger S → smaller δ-to-S ratio → bigger n."}],final:"Bigger S → bigger n → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — more spread means more uncertainty, which needs more data, not less.","Incorrect — noisier data tend to give larger p-values for the same difference.","Incorrect — practical value depends on the economics, not on the spread of the data.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 290, 299"},{id:"sv-3-747",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does the book recommend after obtaining any statistically significant result?",options:["Do a reality check on whether the size of the difference matters","Lower α and repeat the test until p is below 0.01","Report the p-value as the size of the improvement","Move the X straight into the control plan"],answer:0,hint:"Significant is not the same as important.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'The book warns that a statistically significant difference may not be practically significant: "do a reality check."'},{title:"Apply",body:"Look at the estimated difference and its CI and compare it with the difference the business needs."}],final:"Reality check on the size → practical significance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — α is set before the test; re-testing to chase p is poor practice.","Incorrect — p measures evidence against H₀, not the size of the effect.","Incorrect — the X must still pass the practical and root-cause checks and be improved first."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-748",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which description fits a result that is **practically important** but **not statistically significant**?",options:["A real difference that is too small to be worth acting on","A difference that may matter but has not been shown to be real","A real difference that is worth acting on at once","A difference that the test has proved to be zero"],answer:1,hint:"Place the result in the book's two-by-two table.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Not statistically significant = not shown to be real. Practically important = would be worth it if real."},{title:"Apply",body:"So the difference looks worth having but is unproven — check power and collect more data."}],final:"Looks worth it, not proven → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is statistically significant but not practically significant.","","Incorrect — that needs both statistical and practical significance.","Incorrect — a non-significant test never proves the difference is zero."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-749",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Besides the mean, in which other parameter does the book say a practically significant difference can occur?",options:["The p-value of the test","The α level chosen by the team","The variance, or spread, of the process","The number of appraisers in the MSA"],answer:2,hint:"Capability improves by moving the mean or by reducing what?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: the needed difference can be in the **mean** or in the **variance**."},{title:"Apply",body:"A cut in spread can be worth money even when the mean does not move."}],final:"Mean or variance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the p-value is a test result, not a process parameter that can improve.","Incorrect — α is a decision risk the team sets, not a property of the process.","","Incorrect — appraisers belong to measurement system planning, not to the process difference."],bookRef:"Book p. 285"},{id:"sv-3-750",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Why can a test on a **very large** sample flag a trivial difference as statistically significant?",options:["Because a large sample increases the process standard deviation","Because α automatically becomes larger as n increases","Because large samples always contain special causes","Because the standard error shrinks as n grows, so tiny differences stand out from chance"],answer:3,hint:"What happens to the SD of the sample means as n grows?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"SE = s ÷ √n. As n grows, SE falls, so even a very small δ is many standard errors from zero."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is why the book says the outcome can be changed simply by changing the sample size."}],final:"Smaller SE → tiny differences become significant → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — n does not change the process SD; it only reduces the SD of the means.","Incorrect — α is fixed by the team before the test, whatever the sample size.","Incorrect — special causes are a stability issue, unrelated to how significance works.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-751",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What is the PRIMARY reason for agreeing the practical difference before sizing a test?",options:["So the statistical test is designed to detect the difference that matters to the business","So the p-value can be worked out without any sample data","So α can be raised later if the result is not significant","So the team can skip the measurement system analysis"],answer:0,hint:"The statistical difference tested should match what…?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: the team agrees the practical difference in Measure, and the statistical difference tested must match it."},{title:"Apply",body:"The sample size is then chosen so the test can see that difference."}],final:"Match the test to the difference that matters → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a p-value always needs sample data.","Incorrect — α must not be changed after the result is known.","Incorrect — MSA is still needed; the practical difference does not replace it."],bookRef:"Book p. 285"},{id:"sv-3-752",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which part of a test output shows the **size** of a difference, not just whether it is real?",options:["The p-value","The estimate of the difference and its confidence interval","The α level","The degrees of freedom"],answer:1,hint:"Where on the output is a difference given in the units of Y?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The estimate (and its CI) gives the difference in real units — minutes, %, Mbps."},{title:"Apply",body:"Compare that estimate with the practical difference to judge practical significance."}],final:"Estimate + CI show the size → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p shows the strength of evidence against H₀, not how big the difference is.","","Incorrect — α is the team's chosen risk, not a result.","Incorrect — degrees of freedom depend on n and say nothing about the size of the effect."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-753",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about statistical and practical significance is CORRECT?",options:["A statistically significant difference is always worth the cost of the change","Practical significance is shown by a p-value below α","A difference can be statistically significant without being practically significant","A difference that is not statistically significant has no practical value"],answer:2,hint:"Recall the book's warning sentence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Book: "it is possible to realise a statistically significant difference without realising a practically significant difference."'},{title:"Apply",body:"The two ideas answer different questions: is it real, and does it matter?"}],final:"Significant ≠ important → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book warns the reverse; a real difference can be too small to pay back.","Incorrect — a p-value below α shows statistical significance only.","","Incorrect — it may matter but be unproven, often because the sample was small."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-754",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about sample size and significance is CORRECT?",options:["Sample size has no effect on whether a difference is statistically significant","A smaller sample makes it easier to detect a small difference","Increasing the sample size changes the true difference between the means","The outcome of a test can be changed simply by changing the sample size"],answer:3,hint:'The book: "We can affect the outcome of a statistical test simply by…"',steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Same true difference, different n → different p-value, because the standard error changes."},{title:"Apply",body:"This is why the practical difference and the sample size must be planned together."}],final:"n changes the outcome, not the truth → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book stresses that detection is directly linked to sample size.","Incorrect — smaller samples have larger standard errors and miss small differences.","Incorrect — the true difference stays the same; only the uncertainty shrinks.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284, 290"},{id:"sv-3-755",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about the **delta-to-sigma** ratio is CORRECT?",options:["When delta is large compared with sigma, a small sample is enough to show the difference","When sigma is large compared with delta, a smaller sample is needed","The ratio matters only for proportion tests, not for tests of means","A larger delta always requires a larger sample to detect"],answer:0,hint:"Large δ relative to S → obvious difference.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: large δ compared with S → the difference is obvious and a small sample is enough; large S → larger samples."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the first option keeps the direction right."}],final:"Large δ ÷ S → small n → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — noisy data relative to δ need a larger sample, not a smaller one.","Incorrect — the book uses δ and S for tests of means and t-test sample sizes.","Incorrect — the larger the delta, the smaller the sample needed."],bookRef:"Book pp. 290, 299"},{id:"sv-3-756",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about the book's quick rule for p-values is CORRECT?",options:["A p-value above 0.05 means the null hypothesis has been proved true","A p-value above 0.05 means no difference or relationship has been shown","A p-value below 0.05 means the difference is practically important","A p-value below 0.05 means the alternative hypothesis is certainly true"],answer:1,hint:"p > 0.05 → ?, p < 0.05 → ?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book p. 287: p > 0.05 → no difference or relationship shown; p < 0.05 → there is a difference or relationship."},{title:"Apply",body:'"Shown" is the key word — nothing is proved either way.'}],final:"p > 0.05 → nothing shown → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — failing to reject H₀ never proves it.","","Incorrect — p below 0.05 shows statistical significance only.","Incorrect — rejecting H₀ still carries an α risk of being wrong."],bookRef:"Book p. 287"},{id:"sv-3-757",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about **practical significance** are CORRECT?

1. It is the difference needed to pay for the cost of making the change.
2. The team should agree it before the sample size is set.
3. It is shown whenever the p-value is below α.
4. It does not depend on the economics of the process.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Practical = economic value, agreed up front.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Practical significance is the improvement that pays for itself, agreed in Measure before sizing the test."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 1 and 2 are true; 3 confuses it with statistical significance; 4 denies its economic basis."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: p below α shows statistical, not practical, significance.","Incorrect — both are false: practical significance is a business judgement about economic value.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: practical significance is defined by economic or technical value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-758",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about testing with a **very large sample** are CORRECT?

1. It can make a trivial difference statistically significant.
2. It raises the risk of a Type I error above the chosen α.
3. It turns the p-value into a measure of effect size.
4. It shrinks the standard error of the mean.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Large n shrinks the SE — what follows?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"SE = s ÷ √n falls as n rises, so tiny differences become significant."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 1 and 4 are true; α stays where it was set, and p still is not an effect size."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: α is fixed by the team and does not grow with n.","Incorrect — both are false: α is fixed, and p never measures effect size.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: the p-value is never an effect size, whatever n is.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-759",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A difference that probably did not occur by chance is called [?] significant.`,options:["economically","practically","statistically","technically"],answer:2,hint:"Which kind of significance is about chance?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Statistical significance = a change that probably did not happen by chance."},{title:"Apply",body:"Economic, practical and technical all describe value, not chance."}],final:"Not by chance → statistically → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — economic value describes practical significance, not chance.","Incorrect — practical significance asks whether the difference is worth the cost.","","Incorrect — technical value is part of practical significance in the book's definition."],bookRef:"Book p. 269"},{id:"sv-3-760",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Our ability to detect a difference is directly linked to [?] size.`,options:["batch","population","sample","specification"],answer:2,hint:"What changes the standard error?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Book: "Our ability to detect a difference is directly linked to sample size."'},{title:"Apply",body:"SE = s ÷ √n, so n sets how small a difference can be detected."}],final:"Linked to sample size → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — batch size is a production setting, not a driver of test sensitivity.","Incorrect — tests use samples because the population is too large or costly to measure.","","Incorrect — specification width is the VOC, unrelated to detecting a difference in a test."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-761",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

When the sample standard deviation is large compared with delta, [?] samples are needed to establish a difference.`,options:["fewer","larger","paired","smaller"],answer:1,hint:"Noise versus signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: a large S makes differences hard to establish, so larger samples are needed to reduce uncertainty."},{title:"Apply",body:"Large S relative to δ → larger n."}],final:"Large S → larger samples → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — noisy data need more data, not fewer.","","Incorrect — pairing is a design choice for before/after units, not the book's answer to large S.","Incorrect — smaller samples would make the noisy difference even harder to see."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-762",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Each of the following helps judge whether a significant result is **practically** significant EXCEPT:",options:["The estimated size of the difference","The confidence interval for the difference","The p-value of the test","The cost of making the change"],answer:2,hint:"Which item measures evidence, not size or value?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Practical significance compares the size of the difference with its value and cost."},{title:"Apply",body:"The p-value shows only whether the difference is likely real, so it does not help judge practical value."}],final:"p-value is not a practical measure → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the estimate is the main input to practical significance.","Incorrect — the CI shows how large the true difference could plausibly be.","","Incorrect — practical significance is whether the gain pays for this cost."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-763",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a correct interpretation of **p = 0.003** at α = 0.05?",options:["H₀ is rejected","The difference is statistically significant","A difference this large would be unlikely by chance if H₀ were true","The difference is large enough to justify the change"],answer:3,hint:"What can a p-value NOT tell you?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A small p shows the difference is unlikely to be chance — nothing about its size or value."},{title:"Apply",body:"Judging whether the change is worth it needs the estimate, the CI and the business case."}],final:"Small p ≠ worth it → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is correct: 0.003 is below 0.05.","Incorrect — this is correct: p < α means statistical significance.","Incorrect — this is correct: it is the meaning of a small p-value.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-764",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which example would NOT normally be described as **practically** significant?",options:["A 0.2-second fall in mean call length when calls last about 5 minutes","A rise in first-pass yield from 96% to 99%","A cut in billing errors that saves more than the change costs","A fall in repair time large enough to meet a customer SLA"],answer:0,hint:"Would anyone pay for this change?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Practical significance = economic or technical value; the book's example is yield 96% → 99%."},{title:"Apply",body:"0.2 s on a 5-minute call is negligible to customers and the business, even if a large study found it significant."}],final:"Negligible change → not practical → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this is the book's own example of a practically significant difference.","Incorrect — paying back its cost is the book's test of practical significance.","Incorrect — meeting a customer requirement has clear business value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 269, 285"},{id:"sv-3-765",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Each of the following statements about a non-significant result (**p > α**) is correct EXCEPT:",options:["It means H₀ is not rejected","It shows the two populations have identical means","It may reflect a sample that was too small","It means no difference has been shown at that α"],answer:1,hint:"Absence of evidence is not…",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Failing to reject H₀ means there is not enough evidence of a difference — never proof of equality."},{title:"Apply",body:'"Identical means" claims proof, so it is the incorrect statement.'}],final:"Not significant ≠ identical → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is correct: p above α → fail to reject H₀.","","Incorrect — this is correct: small samples often miss real differences.","Incorrect — this is correct: it is the book's quick-rule reading."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-766",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Two screen layouts for order entry are compared. The new layout cuts mean entry time by **25 s** on a 3-minute task (p = 0.001). Clerks enter **100,000** orders a year, so the saving is thousands of hours, and the change costs very little. How should the result be classified?",options:["Statistically but not practically significant","Practically but not statistically significant","Statistically and practically significant","Neither statistically nor practically significant"],answer:2,hint:"Ask two questions: real? worth it?",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.001 < 0.05 → the difference is real."},{title:"Practical",body:"25 s × 100,000 orders at little cost → clear economic value → act on it."}],final:"Real and worth it → both → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the saving is large compared with the low cost, so it matters.","Incorrect — p = 0.001 is far below 0.05, so it is statistically significant.","","Incorrect — both tests of significance are passed here."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-767",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt must decide what difference in mean provisioning time the planned test should be able to detect. What should set this difference?",options:["The p-value of an earlier pilot test","The largest difference seen in the historical data","The default difference shown in the software dialog","The smallest change the business would consider worth acting on"],answer:3,hint:"Delta comes from the business, not the statistics.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: ask how small a change would still be worth acting on — that is the practical difference."},{title:"Apply",body:"The statistical test is then sized to detect that difference."}],final:"Practical need sets δ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a p-value is a test result, not a statement of what difference matters.","Incorrect — history shows what happened, not what change would be worth paying for.","Incorrect — software defaults know nothing about the business case.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 285"},{id:"sv-3-768",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Two network vendors are compared for mean latency. The test shows vendor B is **0.4 ms** faster (p = 0.002). The customer SLA tolerates latency changes up to **10 ms**, and switching vendor would cost **1 million dollars**. What is the MOST appropriate conclusion?",options:["The difference is real but too small to justify switching","The difference is real and justifies switching at once","The difference is not real because the gain is so small","The test is invalid because p is lower than expected"],answer:0,hint:"Is it real? Is it worth 1 million dollars?",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.002 < 0.05 → the 0.4 ms difference is real."},{title:"Practical",body:"0.4 ms is negligible against a 10 ms tolerance; the cost is not paid back → statistically, not practically, significant."}],final:"Real but not worth it → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a 0.4 ms gain is trivial against a 10 ms tolerance and a 1-million-dollar cost.","Incorrect — p = 0.002 < 0.05, so the difference is statistically significant.","Incorrect — a low p-value does not make a test invalid."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-769",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:`In Analyze, three candidate X's for **repair time** are tested:

| X | p-value | Estimated effect |
|---|---|---|
| Spare-parts delay | 0.01 | 45 min per job |
| Technician shift | 0.02 | 0.5 min per job |
| Weather | 0.30 | 20 min per job |

Which X should move on to the root-cause check FIRST?`,options:["Technician shift","Spare-parts delay","Weather","All three, because each has an estimated effect"],answer:1,hint:"Book roadmap: statistically significant? then practically significant?",steps:[{title:"Statistical check",body:"Spare parts (0.01) and shift (0.02) are significant; weather (0.30) is not."},{title:"Practical check",body:"Only spare parts has an effect large enough to matter, so it goes on to the root-cause check."}],final:"Passes both checks → spare-parts delay → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — significant, but 0.5 min per job has little practical value.","","Incorrect — p = 0.30 > 0.05, so the effect has not been shown to be real.","Incorrect — an X must be statistically and practically significant before moving on."],bookRef:"Book p. 238"},{id:"sv-3-770",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:'A team plans to test whether a new triage step cuts mean ticket time by **1 minute**; the process SD is about **4 minutes**. A colleague says, *"Just use 10 tickets per group, like last time."* What is the BEST response?',options:["Ten per group is always enough for a 2-sample t-test","Use 10 per group and raise α if the result is not significant","The sample size depends on this study's delta and SD, so it must be worked out again","Sample size only matters for attribute data, so 10 is fine"],answer:2,hint:`"How many samples?" — the book's answer is…`,steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Book: "How many samples? It depends on your delta and standard deviation."'},{title:"Apply",body:"Here δ is small compared with S, so the needed n is likely far larger than last time."}],final:"n depends on δ and S → recalculate → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — no fixed n suits every study; it depends on δ, S, α and power.","Incorrect — α must be set before the test, not adjusted to fit the result.","","Incorrect — continuous tests are just as dependent on n."],bookRef:"Book p. 299"},{id:"sv-3-771",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A manager reads a report: *"p = 0.0001, so the new call routing gives a huge improvement."* What should the Black Belt point out?',options:["A p-value this small means the test must be repeated","A p-value this small proves the routing is the root cause","A small p-value means a Type II error has occurred","A small p-value shows the difference is likely real, not that it is large"],answer:3,hint:"What does p measure — evidence or size?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"p is the chance of the result if H₀ were true; it is not an effect size."},{title:"Apply",body:'Ask for the estimated reduction and its CI to see whether the gain is "huge".'}],final:"p shows evidence, not size → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a small p-value is not a reason to doubt or repeat a test.","Incorrect — a test proves nothing, and causation needs further evidence.","Incorrect — a Type II error is failing to reject a false H₀; here H₀ was rejected.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-772",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:`Before collecting data for a test of mean fault-repair time, the Black Belt asks the process owner what reduction would justify the investment, and then sizes the sample to detect it.

Was this an appropriate action?`,options:["Yes, because the practical difference should be agreed first and the test matched to it","Yes, because this guarantees the test will be statistically significant","No, because practical significance is judged only after the test","No, because every test should use a fixed sample of 30"],answer:0,hint:"When does the book want the practical difference agreed?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: agree the practical difference in Measure; the statistical difference tested must match it."},{title:"Apply",body:"Asking the owner, then sizing n for that difference, is exactly that."}],final:"Agree δ, then size n → Yes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — planning sets the power, but no plan guarantees a significant result.","Incorrect — the book has the team agree the practical difference in Measure, before testing.","Incorrect — n depends on δ and S; 30 is a CLT rule of thumb, not a sizing rule."],bookRef:"Book p. 285"},{id:"sv-3-773",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:`A pilot shows a significant cut in mean repair time (p = 0.03). The estimate is **4 min**, the business needs at least **3 min**, but the 95% CI is **(0.5, 7.5) min**. The Black Belt recommends collecting more data before a 2-million-dollar rollout.

Was this an appropriate action?`,options:["Yes, because a p-value of 0.03 is not statistically significant","Yes, because the CI shows the true cut could be well below the 3 min needed","No, because the estimate of 4 min already exceeds the 3 min needed","No, because a significant result guarantees the full gain will be achieved"],answer:1,hint:"Is the size of the gain pinned down well enough?",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.03 < 0.05 → the cut is real."},{title:"Practical",body:"The CI runs from 0.5 to 7.5 min, so the true cut may not reach 3 min; more data would narrow the interval before a large investment."}],final:"Real, but practical value uncertain → Yes → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 0.03 is below 0.05, so the cut is statistically significant.","","Incorrect — the estimate is uncertain; the CI includes values far below 3 min.","Incorrect — significance shows the cut is real, not how large it is."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-774",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A test of a new cable-splicing method gives a statistically significant reduction in signal loss (p = 0.01). What should the Black Belt do FIRST?",options:["Check whether the size of the reduction is large enough to matter","Write the control plan for the new method","Collect more data to drive the p-value even lower","Repeat the test at α = 0.10 to confirm it"],answer:0,hint:"Book roadmap after 'statistically significant?'",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's next question is 'practically significant?' — a reality check on the size."},{title:"Apply",body:"Compare the estimated reduction and its CI with the reduction the business needs."}],final:"Next check: practical significance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — control comes after the X is confirmed as worth improving.","Incorrect — the result is already significant; a lower p adds nothing about value.","Incorrect — a looser α cannot confirm anything and was not the agreed risk."],bookRef:"Book p. 238"},{id:"sv-3-775",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A pilot at **8 sites** shows a promising **12%** fall in dropped calls, worth a large saving, but the test gives **p = 0.18**. The sponsor wants to drop the idea. What is the BEST action?",options:["Drop the idea, because the test proved it has no effect","Check the test's power and collect a larger sample before deciding","Roll it out, because a 12% fall is clearly real","Report the result as significant at α = 0.20"],answer:1,hint:"Practically important, not statistically significant, small n.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A promising difference with a small sample may be missed because the test lacks power (book: Boris and Igor)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Check power for the difference that matters and enlarge the sample before deciding."}],final:"Unproven but promising → more data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p > α means not proven, not proven to have no effect.","","Incorrect — with p = 0.18 the difference has not been shown to be real.","Incorrect — α must be set in advance, not chosen to fit the p-value."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-776",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team compares mean **fibre-repair time (min)** before and after a new fault-locator. The business needs a cut of at least **2.5 min**.

\`\`\`
Two-Sample T-Test and CI: Before, After

         N   Mean  StDev
Before  18  24.50   4.60
After   18  18.30   4.60

Difference = μ(Before) − μ(After)
Estimate for difference:  6.20
95% CI for difference:  (3.08, 9.32)
T-Value = 4.04  P-Value = 0.000  DF = 34
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The cut is statistically significant, but it is too small to be practically useful","The cut is not statistically significant because the CI is wide","The cut is statistically significant, and the whole CI lies above the 2.5 min needed","The cut is practically useful, but significance cannot be judged without α = 0.10"],answer:2,hint:"Check p for 'real', then compare the CI with the needed cut.",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.000 < 0.05 and the CI (3.08, 9.32) excludes 0 → significant."},{title:"Practical",body:"The lowest plausible cut, 3.08 min, is above 2.5 min → practically significant as well."}],final:"Significant and CI above the need → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — even the lower CI limit, 3.08 min, exceeds the 2.5 min needed.","Incorrect — the CI excludes 0 and p = 0.000, so the result is significant.","","Incorrect — p = 0.000 is below any usual α, including 0.05."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-777",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Twelve engineers each rate a network alarm before and after a new dashboard; the team believes a change of **0.3** points or more would matter.

\`\`\`
Paired T-Test and CI: After, Before

             N   Mean  StDev  SE Mean
Difference  12  0.230  0.450    0.130

95% CI for mean difference: (-0.056, 0.516)
T-Test of mean difference = 0 (vs ≠ 0): T-Value = 1.77  P-Value = 0.104
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["Not significant, so the dashboard has been shown to make no difference","Significant, because the mean difference of 0.230 is above zero","Practically significant, because the CI upper limit is above 0.3","Not significant, but the CI still includes differences that would matter; more data are needed"],answer:3,hint:"A CI that contains 0 and also contains important values is inconclusive.",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.104 > 0.05; the CI (−0.056, 0.516) contains 0 → fail to reject H₀."},{title:"Practical",body:"The CI also contains values above 0.3, so a useful effect cannot be ruled out. The book notes more samples would narrow the interval."}],final:"Inconclusive → collect more data → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a non-significant test never shows zero difference; the CI reaches 0.516.","Incorrect — p = 0.104 > 0.05 and the CI contains 0, so it is not significant.","Incorrect — an upper limit above 0.3 shows only that a useful effect is possible, not proven.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 285, 336"},{id:"sv-3-778",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team can afford only **20** units for a 1-sample t-test. Minitab *Power and Sample Size* (α = 0.05, power 0.90, σ = 1) shows the smallest difference that can be detected:

\`\`\`
Sample Size   Difference
    10          1.15
    15          0.90
    20          0.76
    30          0.61
    40          0.53
\`\`\`

The practical difference that matters is **0.5** SD. Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["With 20 units the test cannot reliably detect 0.5 SD, so a non-significant result would be weak evidence","With 20 units the test will reliably detect any difference of 0.5 SD or more","The practical difference should be raised to 0.76 SD to fit the budget","The table shows that 10 units would detect 0.5 SD"],answer:0,hint:"Read the detectable difference in the row for n = 20.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"n = 20 → detectable difference 0.76 SD, larger than the 0.5 SD that matters."},{title:"Apply",body:"Even n = 40 (0.53 SD) is not quite enough, so with 20 units a real 0.5 SD effect could easily be missed."}],final:"Detectable 0.76 > needed 0.5 → too insensitive → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — at n = 20 only differences of about 0.76 SD are detected with 90% power.","Incorrect — the practical difference comes from the business, not from the budget.","Incorrect — at n = 10 the detectable difference is 1.15 SD, further away still."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-779",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team tests whether the on-time delivery rate is different from the **90%** target:

\`\`\`
Test and CI for One Proportion
Test of p = 0.9 vs p ≠ 0.9

Sample     X     N   Sample p        95% CI
1       1872  2000   0.936000  (0.925273, 0.946727)

Z-Value = 5.37  P-Value = 0.000
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["The rate is not significantly different from 90%, because the CI is narrow","The rate differs significantly from 90%, and the CI shows it is about 92.5% to 94.7%","H₀ is accepted, because the sample rate is close to the target","The test proves that exactly 93.6% of all deliveries are on time"],answer:1,hint:"Is 0.90 inside or outside the interval?",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.000 < 0.05 and 0.90 lies outside (0.925, 0.947) → reject H₀."},{title:"Size",body:"The CI shows how big the gain plausibly is — the input to the practical judgement."}],final:"0.90 outside CI → significant; CI gives the size → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a narrow CI that excludes 0.90 shows a significant difference.","",'Incorrect — p = 0.000 → reject H₀; and "accept H₀" is never correct wording.',"Incorrect — 93.6% is a sample estimate with an interval around it; nothing is proved."],bookRef:"Book p. 400"},{id:"sv-3-780",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Mean **call duration (s)** is compared across three sites using **4,000** calls per site:

\`\`\`
One-way ANOVA: Duration versus Site

Source     DF        SS     MS      F      P
Site        2     38747  19373  12.11  0.000
Error   11997  19195200   1600
Total   11999  19233947

S = 40.00   R-Sq = 0.20%

Level    N    Mean
A     4000  310.2
B     4000  314.6
C     4000  312.5
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["All three site means differ greatly, so each site needs its own improvement","No site difference exists, because R-Sq is only 0.20%","At least one site mean differs, but the differences are tiny and may not matter in practice","Site is the main driver of call duration, because p = 0.000"],answer:2,hint:"p says whether; the size of the means (and R-Sq) says how much.",steps:[{title:"Statistical",body:"p = 0.000 < 0.05 → at least one site mean differs."},{title:"Practical",body:"The means differ by about 4 s on calls of about 5 minutes, and site explains 0.20% of the variation → probably not practically significant."}],final:"Significant with huge n, but trivial → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — ANOVA shows at least one mean differs; the means are only a few seconds apart.","Incorrect — R-Sq measures how much variation is explained, not whether a difference exists; p = 0.000.","","Incorrect — site explains only 0.20% of the variation; a small p is not a large effect."],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-781",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these planning and review steps for a test of a process change in the correct order:

1. Run the test on the collected data
2. Agree with the sponsor the difference worth detecting
3. Judge whether the estimated difference is large enough to matter
4. Calculate the sample size needed to detect that difference`,options:["4, 2, 1, 3","2, 1, 3, 4","2, 4, 3, 1","2, 4, 1, 3"],answer:3,hint:"Practical need → sample size → test → reality check.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The practical difference is agreed first; n is sized to detect it; then the test runs."},{title:"Apply",body:"Finally the estimate is compared with the practical need: 2 → 4 → 1 → 3."}],final:"2, 4, 1, 3 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the sample size cannot be calculated before the difference to detect is agreed.","Incorrect — sizing the sample after the test is too late; it must come before data collection.","Incorrect — the practical judgement uses the test's estimate, so it comes after the test.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 284–292"},{id:"sv-3-782",phase:3,topic:"3.3.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put these steps for reading a hypothesis-test output in the correct order:

1. State whether H₀ is rejected
2. Compare the p-value with the α chosen in advance
3. Decide whether the difference matters to the business
4. Read the p-value from the output`,options:["4, 2, 1, 3","2, 4, 1, 3","4, 1, 2, 3","4, 2, 3, 1"],answer:0,hint:"Statistical solution first, practical solution last.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Read p → compare with α → reject or fail to reject → practical conclusion."},{title:"Apply",body:"That is 4 → 2 → 1 → 3, mirroring the book's statistical-then-practical steps."}],final:"4, 2, 1, 3 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the p-value must be read before it can be compared with α.","Incorrect — the reject decision follows from the comparison with α, not before it.","Incorrect — the statistical decision comes before the practical judgement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 287–288"},{id:"sv-3-783",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is a **Type I error**?",options:["Failing to reject H₀ when it is actually false","Rejecting H₀ when it is actually true","Rejecting H₀ when it is actually false","Failing to reject H₀ when it is actually true"],answer:1,hint:"Type I = a false alarm.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:'Type I error: you reject a true H₀ — you say "different" when nothing changed.'},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Failing to reject a false H₀ is Type II; the other two are correct decisions."}],final:"Reject a true H₀ = Type I → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is a Type II error (β).","","Incorrect — that is a correct decision; its probability is the power.","Incorrect — that is a correct decision, with probability 1 − α."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-784",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"At which of the book's six hypothesis-test steps are **α and β** chosen?",options:["State the statistical problem","Establish the sample size","Select the test and the risk levels","State the statistical solution"],answer:2,hint:"The risks are set before sample size and data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Step 3 of the book's six steps: select the test and the risk levels (usually α = 0.05, β = 0.10)."},{title:"Apply",body:"It follows the statistical problem and comes before sizing the sample."}],final:"Step 3: test and risk levels → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this step writes H₀ and Hₐ; the risks come next.","Incorrect — sample size uses α and β, so they must already be chosen.","","Incorrect — this step reports reject or fail to reject, after the data are in."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-785",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In the book's words, the **alpha risk** is the risk of:",options:["Failing to recognise a real improvement","Measuring the wrong characteristic of the process","Choosing a sample that is not random","Implementing a change when you should not"],answer:3,hint:"α = acting on a false alarm.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:`Book: α, the producer's risk, is "the risk of implementing a change when you should not."`},{title:"Apply",body:"It follows from rejecting a true H₀ and acting on it."}],final:"α = implementing a change wrongly → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is the beta risk (Type II error).","Incorrect — that is a validity problem, not a decision risk.","Incorrect — that is sampling bias, which α does not cover.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-786",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which term names the **Type I risk** that the team sets before running a test?",options:["Level of significance","Power of the test","Confidence interval","Standard error"],answer:0,hint:"α has a formal name in every test report.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"α is called the **level of significance**: the accepted risk of a Type I error."},{title:"Apply",body:"It is chosen in advance (0.05, 0.10 or 0.01) and compared with the p-value."}],final:"α = level of significance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — power is 1 − β, the chance of detecting a real difference.","Incorrect — a CI is a range estimate calculated from the data.","Incorrect — the standard error is the SD of the sample means."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-787",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the **power** of a hypothesis test?",options:["The probability of rejecting a true H₀","The probability of correctly rejecting a false H₀","The probability of failing to reject a false H₀","The probability that H₀ is true"],answer:1,hint:"Power = finding a difference that is really there.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Power is the probability of correctly rejecting a false H₀; power = 1 − β."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"α and β are the two error risks; P(H₀ true) is not a test output."}],final:"Power = correctly rejecting a false H₀ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is α, the Type I risk.","","Incorrect — that is β, the Type II risk.","Incorrect — no test gives the probability that H₀ is true."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-788",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which power value does the book normally enter in Minitab's *Power and Sample Size* dialog?",options:["0.05","0.10","0.90","0.95"],answer:2,hint:"The book's default β is 0.10.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book uses β = 0.10, so the power it enters is 0.90."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"0.05 is α, 0.10 is β and 0.95 is the confidence level."}],final:"Book power = 0.90 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 0.05 is the usual α, not the power.","Incorrect — 0.10 is the usual β; power is its complement.","","Incorrect — 0.95 is the confidence level for α = 0.05, not the power."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-789",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"When does the book recommend an α of **0.01**?",options:["In most manufacturing studies","In transactional projects involving human behaviour","When H₀ must be made very hard to reject","Whenever the sample size is very large"],answer:2,hint:"A small α makes rejection harder.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: 0.05 most common, 0.10 transactional, 0.01 rarely — only when H₀ must be very hard to reject."},{title:"Apply",body:"Choose 0.01 when a false alarm would be very costly."}],final:"0.01 → H₀ very hard to reject → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book's usual choice there is 0.05.","Incorrect — the book suggests 0.10 for these.","","Incorrect — the book links α to the cost of a false alarm, not to n."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-790",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, which is a likely reason for **failing to recognise a real improvement**?",options:["α was set too high","The sample was very large","The p-value was below α","The sample size was too small"],answer:3,hint:"What drives the β risk up?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: β (failing to recognise an improvement) is usually due to a sample that was too small, or imprecise or inaccurate data."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only a too-small sample matches."}],final:"Too-small sample → Type II error → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a higher α makes rejection easier and lowers β.","Incorrect — a large sample raises power and makes misses less likely.","Incorrect — p below α leads to rejection, so the improvement would be recognised.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 289"},{id:"sv-3-791",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a reference distribution such as t or Z, how is **α** shown?",options:["As the shaded tail area where H₀ is rejected","As the central area between the critical values","As the height of the curve at the mean","As the distance between the two means"],answer:0,hint:"The book calls it the region of doubt.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"α is the shaded tail area (or areas) of the reference distribution; a result there leads to rejecting H₀."},{title:"Apply",body:"In a two-tailed test the area is split between both tails."}],final:"α = shaded rejection tail → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the central area is where H₀ is not rejected (1 − α).","Incorrect — curve height is a density, not a risk.","Incorrect — that distance is delta, the difference to detect."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-792",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does the **consumer's risk** describe?",options:["Saying two things differ when they are really the same","Saying two things are the same when they really differ","Correctly finding a difference that is really there","Correctly concluding there is no difference when none exists"],answer:1,hint:"Consumer's risk = β = a miss.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: β, the consumer's risk, is being wrong when you say two or more things are the same when they are really different."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The reverse mistake is the producer's risk; the other two are correct decisions."}],final:"Consumer's risk = β = missing a difference → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is the producer's risk, α (Type I).","","Incorrect — that is the power of the test.","Incorrect — that is a correct decision, probability 1 − α."],bookRef:"Book p. 289"},{id:"sv-3-793",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"What happens to the **power** of a test if the process standard deviation is reduced, with n, α and the difference unchanged?",options:["Power decreases, because less variation hides the difference","Power is unchanged, because it depends only on α","Power increases, because the difference is easier to see against less noise","Power is unchanged, but α increases"],answer:2,hint:"Think of the δ-to-σ ratio.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Smaller σ → larger δ ÷ σ → the distributions of the means overlap less → smaller β."},{title:"Apply",body:"Power = 1 − β rises."}],final:"Smaller σ → more power → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — less noise makes a fixed difference stand out more, not less.","Incorrect — power depends on n, δ, σ and α.","","Incorrect — α is set by the team and does not move with σ."],bookRef:"Book pp. 289–290"},{id:"sv-3-794",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A team **rejects H₀**. Which error could it have made?",options:["Only a Type II error","Both a Type I and a Type II error","No error, because rejection is certain","Only a Type I error"],answer:3,hint:"Use the decision table: which row is 'reject'?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In the 'reject H₀' row, the only wrong cell is 'H₀ actually true' → Type I error."},{title:"Apply",body:"A Type II error is only possible after failing to reject H₀."}],final:"Reject → only Type I possible → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a Type II error needs a fail-to-reject decision.","Incorrect — one decision can only fall in one column of the decision table.","Incorrect — every decision carries a risk; nothing is proved.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-795",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which three items does the book call the **focus points** of every hypothesis-test decision?",options:["α, β and the sample size n","The mean, the median and the mode","The p-value, the test statistic and the degrees of freedom","Accuracy, precision and stability"],answer:0,hint:"Two risks and one planning quantity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book names α, β and sample size n as the three focus points of every decision."},{title:"Apply",body:"They are linked: for a given difference, n sets the balance of α and β."}],final:"α, β and n → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — these are measures of central tendency.","Incorrect — these are test outputs, not the planning focus points.","Incorrect — these describe a measurement system."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-796",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which value is the PRIMARY yardstick that the p-value is compared with?",options:["β, set before the test","α, set before the test","The power, set before the test","The sample mean, measured in the test"],answer:1,hint:"Reject H₀ if p < ?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: α is the primary decision tool of most tests; reject H₀ if p < α."},{title:"Apply",body:"β and power belong to planning the sample size."}],final:"p is compared with α → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — β is used to size the sample, not to make the decision.","","Incorrect — power is used when sizing the sample, not compared with p.","Incorrect — the sample mean is an estimate, not a decision threshold."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-797",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Compared with a t-test, how does the book say the sample size for a **non-parametric** median test changes?",options:["It is smaller, because ranks need fewer data","It is the same, because α is the same","It is larger, because the median test is less efficient","It is half, because only medians are compared"],answer:2,hint:"Book rule: n for a non-parametric test = t-test n ÷ 0.864.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Dividing by a number below 1 gives a bigger n: median tests are less efficient than t-tests."},{title:"Apply",body:"For the same power, plan more data for a non-parametric test."}],final:"Non-parametric needs a larger n → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — ranking discards information, so more data are needed.","Incorrect — equal α does not mean equal efficiency.","","Incorrect — the book's rule divides by 0.864, which makes n larger."],bookRef:"Book p. 299"},{id:"sv-3-798",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about the **decision table** of a hypothesis test is CORRECT?",options:["It has one correct-decision cell and three error cells","Its two error cells are both called Type I errors","A Type II error sits in the 'reject H₀' row","It has two correct-decision cells and two error cells"],answer:3,hint:"Two decisions × two truths.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Reject/fail to reject × H₀ true/false gives four cells: two correct decisions, one Type I and one Type II error."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the first statement describes the table correctly."}],final:"2 correct + 2 error cells → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both 'reject a false H₀' and 'fail to reject a true H₀' are correct decisions.","Incorrect — one error cell is Type I (α) and the other is Type II (β).","Incorrect — a Type II error is in the 'fail to reject H₀' row, when H₀ is false.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-799",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **producer's** and **consumer's** risks is CORRECT?",options:["The producer's risk is rejecting good product; the consumer's risk is accepting bad product","The producer's risk is accepting bad product; the consumer's risk is rejecting good product","Both names describe the same Type I error","The consumer's risk is always set equal to α"],answer:0,hint:"Who is harmed by each mistake?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"α (Type I) = rejecting a true H₀ = good product rejected → the producer loses. β (Type II) = bad product accepted → the consumer loses."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only the first statement matches each risk to the party it harms."}],final:"Producer: good rejected; consumer: bad accepted → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the labels are swapped; rejecting good product harms the producer.","Incorrect — the producer's risk is α (Type I) and the consumer's risk is β (Type II).","Incorrect — the consumer's risk is β, usually 0.10 in the book, not α."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-800",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about the **α–β trade-off** is CORRECT?",options:["With n fixed, lowering α also lowers β","With n fixed, making H₀ harder to reject makes a real difference easier to miss","Raising α reduces the chance of a false alarm","A larger sample can reduce α but never β"],answer:1,hint:"Two risks, one sample size.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a fixed n, a smaller α (harder to reject) means a bigger β (more misses)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only a larger sample, or less noisy data, cuts both risks at once."}],final:"Harder to reject → easier to miss → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — with n fixed, lowering α raises β.","","Incorrect — raising α increases the chance of a false alarm.","Incorrect — a larger sample is the way to reduce both risks."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-801",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about a test with **very high power** is CORRECT?",options:["It has a very low risk of a Type I error","It guarantees that any significant result is practically important","It is very likely to detect a real difference of the size it was designed for","It makes any difference it finds larger"],answer:2,hint:"Power is about detection of a stated δ.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Power = 1 − β: the chance of correctly rejecting H₀ when the true difference is the δ used in planning."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"α is separate; power says nothing about practical value or the size of the effect."}],final:"High power → likely to detect the planned δ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Type I risk is α, set separately from power.","Incorrect — high power can make trivial differences significant.","","Incorrect — power changes the chance of detection, not the size of the difference."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-802",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about using a gauge with **poor repeatability** in a hypothesis test is CORRECT?",options:["It adds noise, which raises α above the chosen level","It has no effect on the risks, because α is set in advance","It reduces β, because more variation is captured","It adds noise, which raises β and lowers power"],answer:3,hint:"Book: β is often due to imprecise or inaccurate data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Measurement error inflates the observed σ, shrinking δ ÷ σ."},{title:"Apply",body:"Real differences are missed more often: β up, power down — another reason MSA comes before Analyze."}],final:"Noisy gauge → higher β → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — α is set by the team; measurement noise mainly hurts detection.","Incorrect — imprecise data are one of the book's reasons for a Type II error.","Incorrect — extra variation makes real differences harder to see, so β rises.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 289"},{id:"sv-3-803",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the **alpha risk** are CORRECT?

1. It is the probability of missing a real difference.
2. It is the probability of rejecting H₀ when H₀ is true.
3. It is called the producer's risk.
4. It is equal to the power of the test.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"α = Type I = producer's risk.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"α is the probability of rejecting a true H₀, generally called the producer's risk."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 2 and 3 are true; 1 describes β and 4 confuses α with power."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: missing a real difference is β.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: power is 1 − β, not α.","Incorrect — both are false: they describe β and power."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-804",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about **beta** are CORRECT?

1. It is the producer's risk.
2. It is set equal to the p-value of the test.
3. It is the probability of failing to reject a false H₀.
4. Power is equal to 1 − β.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"β = Type II = consumer's risk.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"β is the probability of failing to reject a false H₀; power = 1 − β."},{title:"Apply",body:"Statements 3 and 4 are true; 1 swaps producer and consumer, and 2 confuses β with p."}],final:"Statements 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both are false: β is the consumer's risk, and the p-value is a data result.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: β is a planned risk, not the p-value.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: the producer's risk is α."],bookRef:"Book pp. 289–290"},{id:"sv-3-805",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements about **increasing the power** of a test are CORRECT?

1. A larger sample size increases power.
2. A smaller α increases power.
3. A larger process standard deviation increases power.
4. A larger difference to detect increases power.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"More n, bigger δ, smaller σ, larger α → more power.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Power rises with n and δ, and falls with σ and with a smaller α."},{title:"Apply",body:"Only statements 1 and 4 are true."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: a smaller α lowers power.","Incorrect — both are false: smaller α and larger σ both reduce power.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: more noise makes differences harder to detect.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-806",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The beta risk, or Type II error, is also called the [?] risk.`,options:["consumer's","producer's","sampling","supplier's"],answer:0,hint:"α → producer; β → ?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: β, the Type II error, is also called the consumer's risk."},{title:"Apply",body:"The producer's risk is the other one, α."}],final:"β = consumer's risk → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the producer's risk is α, the Type I error.","Incorrect — sampling error is a source of uncertainty, not the name of β.","Incorrect — 'supplier's risk' is not a term the book uses for either error."],bookRef:"Book p. 289"},{id:"sv-3-807",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

A Type I error is often described as a false [?].`,options:["alarm","economy","negative","start"],answer:0,hint:"Type I = signalling a change that is not there.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Type I error rejects a true H₀: the test raises an alarm when nothing has changed."},{title:"Apply",body:"Missing a real change is the opposite error, a Type II (a false negative)."}],final:"Type I = false alarm → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — 'false economy' is an everyday phrase about saving money, not a test error.","Incorrect — a false negative is a missed real difference, which is a Type II error.","Incorrect — 'false start' has no meaning in hypothesis testing."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-808",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book describes the beta risk as failing to [?] an improvement.`,options:["fund","implement","recognise","standardise"],answer:2,hint:"β = a real improvement that the test does not see.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: beta is failing to recognise an improvement — a real difference is missed."},{title:"Apply",body:"'Implement' belongs to α: implementing a change when you should not."}],final:"β = failing to recognise → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — funding is a business decision, not a statistical risk.","Incorrect — wrongly implementing a change is linked to the alpha risk.","","Incorrect — standardising is a Control activity, not a decision error."],bookRef:"Book p. 289"},{id:"sv-3-809",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a likely consequence of a **Type I error** in a project?",options:["A real improvement is left unused","Money is spent changing an X that does not matter","A change is rolled out that brings no benefit","The team claims a gain that later disappears"],answer:0,hint:"Type I acts on nothing; Type II misses something.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Type I = implementing a change when you should not (book). Type II = failing to recognise an improvement."},{title:"Apply",body:"Leaving a real improvement unused is the consequence of a Type II error."}],final:"Unused real improvement = Type II → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this is a typical Type I consequence: acting on a false alarm.","Incorrect — this follows from wrongly rejecting a true H₀.","Incorrect — a claimed gain that was never real is a Type I outcome."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-810",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Minitab's *Power and Sample Size* works with four quantities and solves for the one left blank. Which is NOT one of them?",options:["Sample size","The p-value","Power","Standard deviation"],answer:1,hint:"Fill in three, leave one blank.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The four quantities: sample size, difference, power and SD (α is set in Options)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A p-value only exists after data are collected, so it is not a planning input."}],final:"p-value is not a sizing input → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — sample size is one of the four, often the one solved for.","","Incorrect — power is one of the four; the book enters 0.90.","Incorrect — SD is one of the four, needed with the difference."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-811",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"With α and the difference to detect unchanged, each of the following happens when the sample size is increased EXCEPT:",options:["β becomes smaller","Power becomes larger","α becomes smaller","The standard error becomes smaller"],answer:2,hint:"Which risk is set by the team rather than by n?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"α is fixed by the team before the test; n does not change it."},{title:"Apply",body:"A larger n shrinks the SE, which lowers β and raises power."}],final:"n does not change α → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this does happen: a larger n reduces the chance of missing a real difference.","Incorrect — this does happen: power = 1 − β rises with n.","","Incorrect — this does happen: SE = s ÷ √n falls as n grows."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-812",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Each of the following is linked to the **β risk** EXCEPT:",options:["Type II error","Consumer's risk","Power of the test","Producer's risk"],answer:3,hint:"Which label belongs to α?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"β = Type II = consumer's risk; power = 1 − β."},{title:"Apply",body:"The producer's risk is α."}],final:"Producer's risk belongs to α → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — β is the probability of a Type II error.","Incorrect — the book calls β the consumer's risk.","Incorrect — power = 1 − β, so it is directly linked.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-813",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A fraud-screening rule tests each card payment with **H₀: the payment is genuine**. A genuine customer's payment is blocked. How is this outcome classified?",options:["A Type I error","A Type II error","A correct decision","The power of the rule"],answer:0,hint:"H₀ true or false? Rejected or not?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Blocking = rejecting H₀. The payment was genuine, so H₀ was true."},{title:"Apply",body:"Rejecting a true H₀ is a Type I error — a false alarm."}],final:"Genuine payment blocked → Type I → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a Type II error would be letting a fraudulent payment through.","Incorrect — H₀ was true and was rejected, so the decision was wrong.","Incorrect — power is correctly blocking a payment that really is fraudulent."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-814",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Incoming batches of SIM cards are accepted or rejected on a sample test. A batch that is actually **defective** passes the test and reaches customers. Which risk has occurred?",options:["Producer's risk (α)","Consumer's risk (β)","Power of the test","Confidence level"],answer:1,hint:"Who suffers when a bad batch is accepted?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Accepting a bad batch = failing to reject a false H₀ = Type II error = consumer's risk."},{title:"Apply",body:"The customer receives the defective product."}],final:"Bad batch accepted → consumer's risk → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the producer's risk is rejecting a good batch.","","Incorrect — power is the correct decision of rejecting a bad batch.","Incorrect — the confidence level is the chance of correctly accepting a good batch."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-815",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:'A sponsor tells the Black Belt: *"If the new dispatch rule really cuts repair time by 2 minutes, I want at least a 90% chance that your pilot shows it."* Which quantity is the sponsor specifying?',options:["The confidence level","The significance level","The power of the test","The p-value"],answer:2,hint:"Chance of detecting a real difference = ?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Power = 1 − β = the probability of rejecting H₀ when a real difference of the stated size exists."},{title:"Apply",body:'"90% chance the pilot shows a real 2-minute cut" is a power of 0.90.'}],final:"90% chance to detect → power → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — confidence (1 − α) concerns not rejecting a true H₀.","Incorrect — significance (α) is the risk of a false alarm.","","Incorrect — the p-value is a result of the data, not a requirement set beforehand."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-816",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Two Green Belts analyse the same fibre-installation data. One planned with α = 0.05, the other with α = 0.10. The test gives **p = 0.07**. Which statement is CORRECT?",options:["Both reject H₀, because 0.07 is close to 0.05","Only the first rejects H₀, because a smaller α is easier to beat","Neither rejects H₀, because p must be below both levels","Only the second rejects H₀, and in doing so accepts a higher risk of a false alarm"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the same p with each α.",steps:[{title:"Apply",body:"0.07 > 0.05 → fail to reject; 0.07 < 0.10 → reject."},{title:"Risk",body:"The larger α means the second Belt accepts a 10% Type I risk instead of 5%."}],final:"Only α = 0.10 rejects, with more Type I risk → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p = 0.07 is above 0.05, so the first fails to reject.","Incorrect — a smaller α is harder to beat; 0.07 is not below 0.05.","Incorrect — each decision uses its own α; 0.07 < 0.10.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-817",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A team tests whether new modem firmware changes dropout rates and **fails to reject H₀**. A large field study later shows the firmware truly has **no** effect. How should the original decision be classified?",options:["A correct decision","A Type I error","A Type II error","A lack of power"],answer:0,hint:"Locate the cell: fail to reject, H₀ true.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Decision table: fail to reject H₀ when H₀ is true → correct decision."},{title:"Apply",body:"There was no effect and the team did not claim one."}],final:"Fail to reject a true H₀ → correct → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a Type I error needs H₀ to be rejected.","Incorrect — a Type II error is failing to reject a false H₀; here H₀ was true.","Incorrect — power matters when H₀ is false; here there was nothing to detect."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-818",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:`Budget limits a pilot to **6 jobs per group**. The sponsor demands α = 0.01 "to be safe". The Black Belt warns that this will make it much harder to detect a real improvement.

Was this an appropriate action?`,options:["Yes, because α = 0.01 also raises the risk of a false alarm","Yes, because with n fixed a smaller α raises β and lowers power","No, because α has no effect on the risk of missing a difference","No, because a smaller α always increases the power of a test"],answer:1,hint:"The α–β trade-off at fixed n.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"With n fixed, making H₀ harder to reject (α = 0.01) makes a real difference easier to miss."},{title:"Apply",body:"With only 6 per group, β would be very high, so the warning is right."}],final:"Fixed n, smaller α → more misses → Yes → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — α = 0.01 lowers the false-alarm risk; the problem is the higher β.","","Incorrect — α and β trade off when n is fixed.","Incorrect — a smaller α decreases power."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-819",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:1,stem:`At the 'select the test and risk levels' step, before any data are collected, the Black Belt sets α = 0.05 and β = 0.10.

Was this an appropriate action?`,options:["Yes, because risk levels are chosen before the data and these are the book's usual values","Yes, because these values guarantee a significant result","No, because α should be chosen after the p-value is known","No, because β is calculated from the p-value"],answer:0,hint:"When, and at what values, does the book set the risks?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Step 3 of the six steps sets the risks in advance, usually α = 0.05 and β = 0.10."},{title:"Apply",body:"The Belt did exactly that."}],final:"Set in advance at book defaults → Yes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — no choice of α and β guarantees a significant result.","Incorrect — choosing α after seeing p biases the decision.","Incorrect — β is a planned risk used to size the sample."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-820",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A team compares **20** different KPIs between two regions, each with a 2-sample test at α = 0.05. One KPI shows p = 0.04 and the team wants to launch a major investigation. What is the BEST response?",options:["Launch the investigation, because p = 0.04 is below α","Treat the single result with caution and confirm it with new data first","Ignore the result, because a p-value of 0.04 is never significant","Lower α for that KPI only, after seeing the result"],answer:1,hint:"Each test carries its own α risk.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Running many tests raises the chance that at least one is a false alarm (overall Type I risk grows)."},{title:"Apply",body:"One 'significant' KPI out of 20 may be chance; confirm it before spending money."}],final:"Many tests → confirm before acting → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — with many tests, one false alarm is quite likely, so the result needs confirming.","","Incorrect — 0.04 is below 0.05; the issue is the number of tests, not the value.","Incorrect — changing α after the result is poor practice."],bookRef:"Book p. 297"},{id:"sv-3-821",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt checks the critical value for a two-tailed Z test at α = 0.05:

\`\`\`
Inverse Cumulative Distribution Function
Normal with mean = 0 and standard deviation = 1

P( X ≤ x )        x
     0.975   1.95996
\`\`\`

Which statement about the values ±1.96 is CORRECT?`,options:["Results beyond ±1.96 fall in the β region, where H₀ is not rejected","Results between ±1.96 lead to rejecting H₀","Results beyond ±1.96 fall in the α region, where H₀ is rejected","Only results above +1.96 can lead to rejecting H₀"],answer:2,hint:"0.975 cumulative → 0.025 in the upper tail, and the same below −1.96.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"1.96 leaves 0.025 above it; by symmetry −1.96 leaves 0.025 below."},{title:"Apply",body:"The two tails together hold α = 0.05 — the rejection region."}],final:"Beyond ±1.96 = α region → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — β is not a tail area of the H₀ distribution; the tails are α.","Incorrect — the central 95% is where H₀ is not rejected.","","Incorrect — that would be a one-tailed test; this test uses both tails."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-822",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Minitab simulation ran a planned test **1,000** times when H₀ was true and **1,000** times when a real difference existed:

\`\`\`
Simulation summary (α = 0.05)
Truth            Runs   % H₀ rejected
H₀ true          1000        4.9%
H₀ false         1000       88.0%
\`\`\`

Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["The estimated β risk is 4.9%","The estimated α risk is 88%","The test is too risky, because it rejects H₀ in most runs","The estimated power of the test is 88%"],answer:3,hint:"Rejecting a true H₀ = α; rejecting a false H₀ = power.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Row 'H₀ true': rejections are Type I errors → about 4.9%, close to α = 0.05."},{title:"Apply",body:"Row 'H₀ false': rejections are correct detections → power ≈ 88%."}],final:"Rejection rate when H₀ is false = power → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 4.9% is the rate of rejecting a true H₀, which estimates α.","Incorrect — 88% is the rate of correctly rejecting a false H₀, which is power.","Incorrect — it rejects mostly when H₀ is false, which is what a good test should do.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-823",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab sizes a pilot:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
2-Sample t Test
Testing mean 1 = mean 2 (versus ≠)
α = 0.05  Assumed standard deviation = 1

            Sample  Target
Difference    Size   Power  Actual Power
       1.5      11     0.9      0.916942

The sample size is for each group.
\`\`\`

Why is the **actual power** higher than the target?`,options:["The sample size is rounded up to a whole number, so the power achieved is a little above 0.90","The test has an α risk of 0.083058","The β risk will be 0.916942","The team must collect 0.916942 extra units per group"],answer:0,hint:"Sample sizes come in whole units.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Minitab finds the smallest whole n that reaches the target power and reports the power that n actually gives."},{title:"Apply",body:"Ten per group is not enough; 11 gives slightly more than 0.90."}],final:"Rounded-up n → actual power above target → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — α is fixed at 0.05; the gap from 1 is β at the actual power.","Incorrect — 0.916942 is the power; β is the small remainder.","Incorrect — power is a probability, not a number of units."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-824",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`With only **15** units per group available, the team asks Minitab for the power to detect a 1-SD difference at three α levels:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
2-Sample t Test, Difference = 1, SD = 1, n = 15 per group

   α    Power
0.10    0.848
0.05    0.753
0.01    0.500
\`\`\`

The sponsor wants α = 0.01. Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["Power rises to 0.848, so a real difference is easier to detect","Power falls to 0.500, so a real 1-SD difference would be missed far more often","Power is unchanged, because n is the same at every α","α = 0.01 reduces both the false-alarm and the missed-difference risks"],answer:1,hint:"Read the row for α = 0.01.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"α = 0.01 → power 0.500, against 0.753 at α = 0.05."},{title:"Apply",body:"At fixed n, a stricter α trades fewer false alarms for more misses."}],final:"Smaller α → lower power → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 0.848 is the power at α = 0.10, not at 0.01.","","Incorrect — the table shows power falling as α gets smaller.","Incorrect — with n fixed, a smaller α raises β."],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-825",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`Minitab output for a planned comparison of two call-centre scripts:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
2-Sample t Test
Testing mean 1 = mean 2 (versus ≠)
α = 0.05  Assumed standard deviation = 1

            Sample  Target
Difference    Size   Power  Actual Power
         1      23     0.9      0.912498

The sample size is for each group.
\`\`\`

Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["23 calls are needed in total, split between the two scripts","Each script needs 23 groups of calls","23 calls are needed for each script","The test has a 91.2% chance of a Type I error"],answer:2,hint:"Read the last line of the output.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:'Sample size 23, "for each group"; actual power 0.912.'},{title:"Apply",body:"For a 2-sample t the size is per group, as the book notes."}],final:"23 per group → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the output states that the sample size is for each group.","Incorrect — 23 is a number of observations, not of groups.","","Incorrect — 0.912 is the power; the Type I risk is α = 0.05."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-826",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab shows the power of a planned 2-sample t-test with **10** units per group for several true differences:

\`\`\`
Power and Sample Size
2-Sample t Test, α = 0.05, SD = 1, n = 10 per group

Difference   Power
       0.5   0.185
       1.0   0.562
       1.5   0.887
       2.0   0.988
\`\`\`

Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["Every difference in the table is detected with at least the book's 90% power","A true difference of 0.5 SD would be detected about 81.5% of the time","The false-alarm risk rises as the difference gets larger","A true difference of 2.0 SD would almost always be detected, but 0.5 SD would usually be missed"],answer:3,hint:"Power rises with the size of the true difference.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Power is 0.988 at 2.0 SD but only 0.185 at 0.5 SD."},{title:"Apply",body:"With n = 10, large differences are found; small ones are mostly missed (high β)."}],final:"Big δ found, small δ missed → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — only 2.0 SD reaches 0.90; 1.5 SD gives 0.887 and smaller differences far less.","Incorrect — 0.185 is the power at 0.5 SD; the rest is β, the chance of missing it.","Incorrect — the false-alarm risk is α = 0.05 throughout; power is what rises.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-827",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"When a team **fails to reject H₀**, the only error it could have made is a Type I error.",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Which row of the decision table is 'fail to reject'?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Fail to reject H₀ when H₀ is false = Type II error (β)."},{title:"Apply",body:"A Type I error is only possible after rejecting H₀, so the statement is false."}],final:"Fail to reject → only Type II possible → False → answer **False**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a Type I error needs H₀ to be rejected; failing to reject can only be a Type II error.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 288–290"},{id:"sv-3-828",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Arrange these situations from the **strictest** α (hardest to reject H₀) to the most lenient, following the book's usual choices:

1. A transactional project driven by human behaviour
2. A typical manufacturing study
3. A change where H₀ must be very hard to reject`,options:["3, 2, 1","2, 3, 1","1, 2, 3","3, 1, 2"],answer:0,hint:"0.01 → 0.05 → 0.10.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: 0.01 when H₀ must be very hard to reject, 0.05 typical (manufacturing), 0.10 transactional."},{title:"Apply",body:"Strictest to most lenient: 3 → 2 → 1."}],final:"3, 2, 1 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the strictest α (0.01) belongs to situation 3, not to manufacturing.","Incorrect — this is the reverse: transactional work uses the most lenient α (0.10).","Incorrect — transactional work (0.10) is more lenient than manufacturing (0.05)."],bookRef:"Book p. 288"},{id:"sv-3-829",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these steps for using Minitab's *Power and Sample Size* in the correct order:

1. Read the value that Minitab solves for
2. Choose the test in *Stat > Power and Sample Size*
3. Enter three of the four quantities and leave one blank
4. Decide the difference that matters and estimate the SD`,options:["2, 3, 4, 1","4, 2, 3, 1","2, 4, 1, 3","4, 3, 2, 1"],answer:1,hint:"Plan the inputs, open the dialog, fill it, read it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The practical difference and SD come from the business and historical data."},{title:"Apply",body:"Then choose the test, fill three boxes, and read the solved fourth: 4 → 2 → 3 → 1."}],final:"4, 2, 3, 1 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the difference and SD must be known before they can be entered.","","Incorrect — Minitab cannot solve anything before the values are entered.","Incorrect — the test must be chosen before its dialog can be filled in."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"},{id:"sv-3-830",phase:3,topic:"3.3.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Three planned 2-sample studies use the same α and the same process SD:

- Study P: 50 per group, difference to detect = 1.0 SD
- Study Q: 50 per group, difference to detect = 0.5 SD
- Study R: 10 per group, difference to detect = 0.5 SD

Arrange them from **highest** to **lowest** power.`,options:["R, Q, P","Q, P, R","P, Q, R","P, R, Q"],answer:2,hint:"Compare pairs that differ in only one driver.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Power rises with n and with the difference to detect."},{title:"Apply",body:"P > Q (same n, bigger δ) and Q > R (same δ, bigger n) → P, Q, R."}],final:"P, Q, R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is the reverse; R has the smallest n and a small difference.","Incorrect — P and Q share n, and P's larger difference gives it more power.","","Incorrect — Q and R share the difference, and Q's larger n gives it more power."],bookRef:"Book p. 290"}];export{e as items};
